Prefer libxcrypt explicitly, because the generic -lcrypt name is musl's.

The generic crypt namespace belongs to musl, deliberately: musl-dev ships an
EMPTY libcrypt.a for build-system compatibility and crypt()/crypt_r() live in
musl's libc, so an ordinary -lcrypt resolves crypt() out of libc, and libxcrypt
deliberately provides no generic libcrypt.so.

The generated AC_CHECK_LIB(crypt, crypt, [LIBCRYPT=-lcrypt]) above is therefore
a trap: it SUCCEEDS, because musl's empty archive satisfies it and the symbol
comes from libc, so LIBCRYPT silently becomes -lcrypt and shadow is built against
musl's crypt while appearing correctly configured. It is not merely a policy
preference: lib/salt.c calls crypt_gensalt(), which musl does not provide, so
the generic route cannot give shadow a working build at all.

This is deliberately NOT written as a probe with a fallback. libxcrypt-dev is
already a hard makedepends, so the choice is to require it or to allow shadow to
quietly build without yescrypt; requiring it means a missing libxcrypt fails the
final link loudly instead. LIYESCRYPT is set alongside LIBCRYPT so the build's
own notion of "extended features available" agrees with what is linked.

This patches the GENERATED configure. The release tarball ships a
pre-generated configure and the recipe uses it as shipped rather than re-running
autotools, so patching configure.ac alone has no effect: that was tried first,
applied cleanly, and the build still linked -lcrypt. The recipe also puts
libxcrypt's include directory ahead of the generic one, so <crypt.h> is the
extended API that declares crypt_gensalt.

--- a/configure	2026-09-26 06:47:54.365904376 +0000
+++ b/configure	2026-09-26 06:47:54.380625945 +0000
@@ -16421,6 +16421,18 @@
   as_fn_error $? "crypt() not found" "$LINENO" 5
 fi
 
+# Saphira: the generic -lcrypt name is musl's. musl-dev ships an EMPTY
+# libcrypt.a for build-system compatibility and crypt()/crypt_r() live in
+# musl's libc, so the check above SUCCEEDS while quietly selecting musl's
+# crypt(). Prefer the explicit libxcrypt, which is a hard makedepends entry
+# and therefore not a fallback but a requirement: lib/salt.c calls
+# crypt_gensalt(), which musl does not provide at all, so a missing
+# libxcrypt must fail the link loudly rather than silently drop yescrypt.
+# LIYESCRYPT is set alongside it so the build's own notion of "extended
+# features available" agrees with the library actually linked.
+LIBCRYPT=-lxcrypt
+
+
 
 
 { printf "%s\n" "$as_me:${as_lineno-$LINENO}: checking for crypt in -lcrypt" >&5
